{% extends 'help/article.html.twig' %}

{% block body %}
    <p>
        A load in Fleet Pro is a <strong>sequence of stops</strong>, not a from-and-to. Even a
        simple run is one pickup and one delivery, each with its own address, appointment window,
        contact and reference number. That shape is what lets a three-stop run be recorded
        properly instead of squeezed into a notes field.
    </p>

    <h2>Stops</h2>
    <p>
        Each stop is a pickup or a delivery, and they run in sequence. A stop carries:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Facility name and address.</strong> The address is geocoded as you type, which is what makes routing and the weather forecast work.</li>
        <li><strong>Date and time window.</strong> Either an appointment — a firm window the receiver has given you — or first-come-first-served.</li>
        <li><strong>Contact and phone</strong> for the person at that facility.</li>
        <li><strong>Reference number</strong> — the PO, BOL or pickup number that facility will ask your driver for.</li>
        <li><strong>Instructions</strong>, which go to the driver, and a <strong>store/DC</strong> number where the receiver uses one.</li>
        <li><strong>Proof of delivery required</strong>, per stop, because some receivers want it and some do not.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Changing a stop's address or date updates the load's own origin, destination and dates,
        and re-runs the route. You do not maintain those separately.
    </p>

    <div class="note">
        <p>Stops are created with the load. You can edit any stop afterwards, but adding or removing
        one is done by editing the load itself rather than from the stop.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Freight details</h2>
    <p>
        These describe what is on the trailer, and several of them do real work later — they are
        what <a href="{{ path('help_article', { slug: 'assigning-drivers' }) }}">driver suggestions</a>
        check against:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Load type</strong> and <strong>trailer type</strong> — a reefer load will not suggest a driver pulling a flatbed.</li>
        <li><strong>Temperature</strong> for temperature-controlled freight.</li>
        <li><strong>Weight</strong> and <strong>pallets or pieces</strong> — weight is checked against trailer payload capacity.</li>
        <li><strong>Commodity</strong>, and the <strong>hazmat</strong> flag, which requires a driver with the endorsement.</li>
        <li><strong>Seal required</strong> and <strong>straps / load bars</strong> for securement.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Rate and cost</h2>
    <p>
        The money on a load is a breakdown, not a single number, and each line has a category that
        decides which side of the ledger it lands on:
    </p>
    <div class="help-scroll">
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Category</th><th>Typical lines</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>Rate addition</td><td>Line haul, stop-off, detention, layover, TONU</td><td>Adds to revenue</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Surcharge</td><td>Fuel surcharge</td><td>Adds to revenue</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Other revenue</td><td>Anything else billable</td><td>Adds to revenue</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Cost</td><td>Tolls, estimated fuel, other costs</td><td>Subtracts</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p>
        From those lines the load shows <strong>total revenue, total cost and profit</strong>. Each
        line also has an <strong>include in rate confirmation</strong> switch, so an internal cost
        estimate stays off the document the driver and broker see.
    </p>

    <div class="warn">
        <p>Rate, cost and profit are four separate permissions. A dispatcher typically sees the rate
        but not the profit — if a number is missing for one of your users, that is why. See
        <a href="{{ path('help_article', { slug: 'users-and-permissions' }) }}">Users and permissions</a>.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Notes, in three places</h2>
    <p>
        There are deliberately three note fields, because mixing them is how the wrong thing ends
        up in front of the wrong person:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Special instructions</strong> — goes to the driver, and onto the confirmation.</li>
        <li><strong>Broker notes</strong> — what the broker told you.</li>
        <li><strong>Internal comments</strong> — for your office, and nowhere else.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Statuses</h2>
    <p>
        A load moves through <strong>pending → assigned → in transit → delivered</strong>, with
        <strong>cancelled</strong> as the exit. Assigning a driver is what moves it off pending.
    </p>

    <h2>Equipment follows the driver</h2>
    <p>
        You assign a <strong>driver</strong> to a load, not a truck. The truck and trailer are
        whatever that driver is currently assigned, so the equipment on a load is always the
        equipment that actually went. If you need a different truck on a run, change the driver's
        assignment on the <a href="{{ path('help_article', { slug: 'trucks-and-trailers' }) }}">truck record</a>
        rather than looking for a truck field on the load.
    </p>
{% endblock %}
